\section{Example SimOpt Problem: Chess Matchmaking (\href{https://simopt.readthedocs.io/en/latest/chessmm.html}{ChessMM})}\label{sec:simoptmoredetails}

\subsection{Problem and Prompt}
This problem involves matching players on an online chess platform to minimize the average Elo difference between matched pairs, while ensuring that the average waiting time does not exceed a specified threshold ({\tt delta}=5.0). In this setting, players arrive according to a Poisson process and their Elo ratings are sampled from a truncated normal distribution over the interval {\tt [0,2400]}. The prompt for this problem is as follows (adapted for better exposition):

\begin{tcolorbox}[simoptprompttemplate, breakable]
You're an expert in stochastic modeling and you're tasked to solve the following problem.
\vspace{0.5em}

------------------------------------------
Objective
------------------------------------------
\vspace{0.5em}

Minimize the average Elo difference between matched players, subject to the constraint that the average waiting time does not exceed a specified threshold delta (or {\tt upper\_time}).
\vspace{0.5em}

------------------------------------------
Problem Description
------------------------------------------
\vspace{0.5em}

We have an online chess platform where players arrive according to a stationary Poisson process with rate lambda. Each player has an Elo rating drawn from a truncated normal distribution with mean 1200 and standard deviation {\tt 1200/(sqrt(2)*erfcinv(1/50))}. This distribution is truncated at 0 and 2400, giving approximately 0 as the 1st percentile and 2400 as the 99th percentile.
\vspace{0.5em}

