\subsection{Generalization to Unseen Datasets}

To evaluate generalization, we tested all methods on three publicly available ultrasound datasets from unseen domains: breast (BUSBRA), thyroid (TNSCUI), and multifidus muscle (Luminous). UniverSeg has an advantage in that We provided it with a 16-image labelled support set from each unseen dataset before inference.  It should also be noted that, as with the seen dataset, random points within the ground truth mask are provided as spatial prompts for SAMUS.

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:unseen}, our method achieves the highest average DSC ($72.83\%$) and IoU ($68.53\%$) across datasets, outperforming all competing approaches without retraining or domain-specific adaptation. On the BUSBRA dataset, our model reached a DSC of $79.10\%$ and IoU of $86.44\%$, exceeding all other methods. For the TNSCUI thyroid dataset, it achieved the highest DSC ($86.44\%$) and IoU ($79.10\%$), showing strong robustness. On the Luminous dataset, while SAMUS slightly outperformed our method in DSC (65.36\% vs 64.99\%), our approach achieved the best IoU ($51.96\%$ vs $49.99\%$), indicating better overlap with the ground-truth masks.

Finally, it is worth noting that SAMUS and BiomedParse benefit from much larger pretraining datasets (30,000 and 3.4 million images, respectively), whereas our model is trained on significantly less data yet generalizes more effectively across diverse anatomical structures and imaging conditions.

