\section{Proposed Method}
We introduce our strategy for discovering a suitable permutation of target token sequences. The key idea is to leverage a characteristic of the training dynamics of deep neural networks: they tend to learn easy samples in the early stages of training, and gradually adapt to harder samples later. This phenomenon has been reported in several contexts in the literature, such as~\citep{Memorization_DNNs} for learning with noisy labels and~\citep{Lens_difficulty} for identifying difficult examples.

We exploit this property by training a Transformer for a few epochs on a dataset with various orders in mixture and identify learning-friendly orders as ``easy samples,'' for which loss drops faster.

More formally, let $D=\{(X_i,Y_i)\}_{i=1}^{m}$ and $D'=\{(X_i',Y_i')\}_{i=1}^{m'}$ be training and validation sets, respectively.
Let $\mathcal{P}=\{P_1,\dots,P_{T}\}$ be the set of $T$ candidate permutation matrices.  Let $D^{P_t}$ be the set $D$ with reordered target sequences by $P_t$, i.e., $D^{P_t} = \{(X_i, Y_iP_t)\}_{i=1}^m$. 
We determine learning-friendly orders through the following loss profiling. 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textbf{P1.}] Let $E\in \bN$. Train a Transformer for $E$ epochs on a mixed dataset $\bar{D} := \bigcup_{t=1}^T D^{P_t}$. Let $\cT_{\theta'}$ be the Transformer after training.
    \item[\textbf{P2.}] Compute the loss on the validation set $D'$ for each permutation; namely, for $t=1,\ldots, T$, compute
    \begin{align}
        \mathcal{L}(D', P_t) = \frac{1}{m^{\prime}} \sum_{i=1}^{m^{\prime}}\ell(\mathcal{T}_{\theta'}, X'_i, Y'_iP_{t}).
    \end{align}
    Then, the most learning-friendly order $P^* := P_{\tau}$ is determined with $\tau = \argmin_{t} \cL(D', P_t)$.
\end{enumerate}

