As large-model agents gain autonomy—capable of long-horizon planning, dynamic tool use, and adaptive behavior—they must balance performance with safety across multiple dimensions. Traditional Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) offer no explicit mechanism to encode operational risks, such as privacy leakage, irreversible actions, or resource overuse. Conversely, heuristic rule filters or ad hoc safety checks provide limited generalization and no guarantees.
The \textit{Constrained Markov Decision Process} (CMDP) framework~\cite{altman1999constrained} offers a principled foundation for reasoning under both reward and constraint. By augmenting the standard MDP with explicit cost functions and budget thresholds, CMDPs enable agents to optimize long-term utility while respecting multi-dimensional safety bounds. The agent seeks a policy $\pi^*$ that maximizes expected cumulative reward while satisfying multiple risk constraints:
\begin{align}
\pi^{*} \;=\; 
\arg\max_{\pi}\;
        \mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\Big[\!\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\!\gamma^{t} R(s_t,a_t)\Big] \nonumber \\
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad 
        \mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\Big[\!\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\!\gamma^{t} C_k(s_t,a_t)\Big]
        \le d_k,\;\forall k. \label{eq:cmdp}
\end{align}

