\subsection{Application to Robust Multi-Objective Optimization}

\subsubsection{Motivation}
Through the RPS experiments, we successfully validated MGM’s efficacy in enhancing coevolutionary stability, overcoming non-transitivity, and evolving high-quality strategies. However, coevolutionary algorithms are not limited to finding optimal strategies; they also encompass the search for robust solutions under uncertainty and perturbations. Robust Multi-Objective Optimization (RMOO) is the field that specifically addresses this challenge. C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D, via competitive coevolution, has successfully identified strong perturbation signals and converged to the Pareto front\cite{Meneghini2016Competitive}. Its philosophy of coevolving perturbations and design variables aligns with the foundational applications of MGM.

Based on the RPS experiments, we found that MGM can proactively seek perturbation signals that exert greater selective pressure on the current population and use these signals as evolutionary anchors to guide population evolution. Therefore, we integrate MGM into the C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D coevolutionary framework and extend the strategy space from discrete to continuous to fully evaluate the applicability of MGM’s core mechanisms.

\subsubsection{Experiment Setting}

We enhanced the coevolutionary component of C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D by incorporating the marker gene update mechanism and the dynamic weight adjustment mechanism in both the $X$ and $\Delta$ populations. We set marker genes in the $X$ and $\Delta$ populations and updated them according to predefined thresholds. 

We conducted ten independent runs on the ZDT-1 and ZDT-2 benchmark functions to ensure statistical significance and reproducibility. For each run, the random seed was set solely based on the run index. To ensure a fair comparison, we retained the baseline settings of the original C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D experiments and only adjusted parameters related to the MGM components. For the evolutionary process, an interval-based crossover operator was utilized, where offspring variables were generated by uniformly sampling from an extended range defined by the parent variables, controlled by a crossover parameter. Mutation was applied probabilistically to each variable with a probability $P_m$,  and it was implemented as an additive uniform perturbation. The specific experimental parameters are listed in Table~\ref{tab:experimental_settings}  

