\subsection{Robustness Against Pathological Dynamics: The Shapley Game}
\label{sec:shapley_game}
The preceding experiments have established that the core MGM framework—comprising the marker gene and dynamic weighting—is sufficient to ensure stable convergence in both standard competitive games (RPS) and complex multi-objective optimization problems (CR-MOEA/D). We now turn to a final, more stringent test: confronting a game with pathological dynamics designed to break co-evolutionary systems.
For this ultimate challenge, we introduce an auxiliary mechanism that was intentionally omitted previously: the Memory Pool (MP). While the core MGM mechanisms provide robust internal stability, the MP acts as an explicit historical archive, creating an additional “gravitational anchor” that provides the overwhelming stabilizing force required to counteract the extreme centrifugal dynamics of pathological games. This section, therefore, evaluates the complete MGM-MP system to demonstrate its power in solving one of the most notoriously difficult benchmarks in the field.

\subsubsection{Motivation}
To push our algorithm to its limits and validate its stability under extreme conditions, we employ the Shapley Biased Game---a benchmark notorious for its pathological intransitive dynamics\cite{shapley1964}. Unlike standard RPS, the Shapley Game induces a strong "centrifugal force" that actively drives evolutionary trajectories \textit{away} from the Nash Equilibrium (NE), making stable convergence a formidable challenge for most CCEAs. We use the canonical non-zero-sum payoff matrix for this game:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:shapley_matrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1 \\
1 & 0 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

The unique NE for this game is the uniform strategy profile $x^* = (1/3, 1/3, 1/3)$. We compare the performance of the MGM with MP algorithm against a standard CCEA baseline and MGM.
\subsubsection{The Memory Pool (MP) Mechanism}
While MGM’s core mechanisms significantly stabilize the evolutionary process, the extreme intransitivity of the Shapley Game can still induce long-term cycling, where the population forgets and re-learns critical past solutions (Fig.~\ref{fig:memory_decrease}). To overcome this final stability challenge and ensure robust convergence, we introduce the Memory Pool (MP) mechanism.
\begin{figure}[htp]
% 't' for top, 'h' for here, 'b' for bottom
    \centering
    % Ensure the path to your figure is correct
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\columnwidth]{pdf/8.png} 
    \caption{Illustration of redundant learning cycles resulting from the decay of marker gene influence over time (as indicated by 'Memory Will Decrease'). This decay can lead to the re-learning of past challenges.}
    \label{fig:memory_decrease}
\end{figure}
The MP operates on two core principles. First, it archives ‘entangled pairs’: previously replaced marker genes bundled with their strongest contemporary adversaries. Before each generation, these pairs are evaluated against the current population and partitioned into an ‘active set’ and an ‘inactive set’. Pairs in the active set are re-injected into the current population to participate in breeding, reintroducing effective historical solutions into the evolutionary search.
Second, this is complemented by a ‘Cemetery’, which serves as a long-term archive for individuals culled from the main pool to manage its size. If the population’s evolutionary progress stagnates, the oldest memory is ‘resurrected’ from the cemetery to reintroduce historical diversity and break the cycle.
Together, the MP and Cemetery enable MGM to effectively recycle historical experience, break intransitive traps, and significantly enhance its long-term stability and convergence when facing pathological dynamics. A detailed description of the MP’s implementation, including specific weight calculations and management policies, is available in the Supplementary Material (Section S.II).
\subsubsection{Experiments Setting and Results}
To demonstrate the dual role of the Memory Pool (MP), we evaluated its impact in both a standard and a pathological game.

In the standard RPS game, the results (Fig.~\ref{fig:mp_effect_combined} ) show that MP acts as a performance accelerator. By preventing the re-exploration of known suboptimal regions, it significantly speeds up convergence and improves final precision compared to the baseline without MP.

The true test of the MP’s utility, however, comes from pathological environments. We therefore applied MGM with MP to the Shapley Biased Game. A key adjustment was made to the setup: we set the fitness threshold to $l=0.3$ to align with the game’s theoretical Nash Equilibrium payoff of $1/3$. Under this condition, the MP’s role transforms from an accelerator to a critical stabilizer (Fig.~\ref{fig:mp_effect_shapley}). It introduces a crucial ratchet effect by “locking in” incremental victories against pathological strategies. This mechanism breaks the powerful intransitive cycle, which the core MGM alone struggles to overcome, and enables the population to robustly converge to the theoretical NE.

In summary, these experiments confirm that the MP is not merely an optional optimization but a vital component of the MGM framework. It enhances efficiency in standard scenarios and provides the necessary robustness to achieve stable convergence in extreme pathological problems.
