\section{Conclusion and Future Work }
\label{sec:V}
The main contributions of this paper are: 1) We propose and design a novel Marker Gene Method (MGM) that effectively addresses the challenges of competitive coevolutionary algorithms through the synergistic integration of marker genes, dynamic weight adjustment.Critically, we introduce a Memory Pool (MP) mechanism as an essential extension to guarantee stability in pathologically challenging environments. 2) We provide rigorous proofs and theoretical analyses of MGM’s convergence, elucidating the role of each component (see Supplementary Materials  I for details).3)We empirically validate MGM’s significant advantages over existing algorithms through a systematic, multi-tiered evaluation:from establishing stable convergence in standard Strictly Competitive Games (RPS) and demonstrating practical utility in complex Pareto-front searches (C-RMOEA/D), to ultimately proving its unique robustness by conquering the notoriously pathological Shapley Biased Game, a feat not achievable without the MP mechanism.

This work presents MGM as a powerful framework to mitigate intransitivity, disengagement, and the ‘Red Queen’ effect. By demonstrating its success from foundational theory and practical application to its ultimate triumph over pathological dynamics, we not only equip competitive coevolutionary algorithms with a robust tool but also offer important insights and formal techniques for understanding, designing, and guaranteeing the stability of complex evolutionary systems.

Although the proposed MGM algorithm demonstrates effectiveness both theoretically and empirically, there remains ample room for future exploration. 1)MGM’s core mechanisms—leveraging marker genes to identify key opponents/samples, dynamically balancing exploration and exploitation, and employing a memory pool—have potential for extension to other evolutionary computation paradigms and machine learning domains. 2)Since the current theoretical analysis is mainly grounded in the Strictly Competitive Game (SCG) framework, a key future direction is to generalize MGM to broader game-theoretic models and investigate its adaptability and convergence under more complex interactions. 3) There is room to improve the MGM algorithm itself. The most critical enhancement is the development of an adaptive strategy for the threshold $l$. Rather than simple heuristics, we envision a more fundamental approach rooted in control theory and information geometry. The core idea is to design a meta-controller that actively guides the evolutionary dynamics. This controller’s objective would be to adjust $l$ in real-time to ensure the system’s underlying vector field maintains negative divergence ($\operatorname{div} F <0$), thus guaranteeing convergence to a stable attractor. Such a mechanism would not only eliminate a key hyperparameter but would also represent a new paradigm for ensuring stability in complex competitive systems, moving beyond MGM to broader applications like GANs and MARL.In summary, we believe that MGM is not merely an algorithm for coevolutionary contexts but a methodology that can be broadly extended to other competitive game-theoretic domains.
