\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Mechanistic Interpretability and Circuits}
Mechanistic interpretability seeks to uncover the computational strategies and representational structures internalized by neural networks, aiming at transparency at the level of individual components such as neurons, attention heads, and weight matrices. The early foundational work by \citet{olah2020zoom} introduced the concept of \textit{circuits}, coherent subgraphs within neural networks that correspond to meaningful algorithmic operations. Subsequent studies by \citet{elhage2021mathematical}  and \citet{wang2022interpretability} expanded this framework, dissecting the internal structures of transformer models and providing interpretive insights into attention mechanisms, feed-forward layers, and embedding spaces. A comprehensive practical review of these advances in mechanistic interpretability, specifically tailored for transformer-based language models, is provided by \citet{rai2024review}.

%whose categorization of techniques such as attention-pattern visualization, neuron activation analysis, and  activation patching forms a methodological foundation for dissecting numerical reasoning circuits in transformers.
\subsection{Modular Exponentiation}
Modular exponentiation, despite its mathematical simplicity, plays a crucial role in cryptography, number theory, and computational complexity \citep{rivest1978method, 1055638}. Classical algorithms such as fast exponentiation use the cyclic nature of modulo arithmetic to efficiently compute large exponentials modulo a number. Machine learning approaches to modular arithmetic have been explored, but remain limited to simpler operations such as modular addition and multiplication \citep{gromov2023grokking, saxena2024modular}. Recent studies such as the work of \citet{gromov2023grokking} demonstrate the ability of neural networks to learn modular addition through \textit{grokking}, a sudden generalization phenomenon. Our work significantly extends this line of research by systematically investigating modular exponentiation, an inherently more complex and non-linear task, from a deep learning perspective.
\subsection{Transformers and Mathematics}
Mechanistic interpretability has recently been applied specifically to arithmetic reasoning within transformer architectures. For instance, \citet{quirke2024understanding} examined transformers performing addition tasks, discovering dedicated attention heads whose functions mirror human-engineered arithmetic algorithms. Similarly, \citet{stolfo2023mechanistic} used causal mediation analysis to rigorously demonstrate interpretable arithmetic pathways within transformer architectures, revealing explicit algorithmic structures encoded in model activations. Extending this line of research, \citet{sharkey2022superposition} used sparse autoencoders to disentangle complex arithmetic features hidden within transformer activations, improving the clarity and interpretability of arithmetic computations in neural models.

A notable phenomenon driving recent mechanistic interpretability studies in arithmetic contexts is grokking --- characterized by delayed and sudden generalization from memorization to genuine algorithmic understanding. Initially documented by \citet{power2022grokking} in the context of modular arithmetic tasks, grokking has provided valuable insights into how neural networks transition abruptly from simple memorization strategies to meaningful generalization. Subsequent analysis, such as that of \citet{nanda2023progress}, introduced internal ``progress measures'' to identify specific circuits responsible for this emergent arithmetic capability. More recently, \citet{doshi2024modular} studied grokking specifically within modular polynomial arithmetic, systematically uncovering specialized internal representations that transformers leverage during modular operations. 

Our work builds directly upon these foundations by extending explainability techniques --- including learning dynamics analysis, activation patching, and PCA on embedding representations --- to the previously unexplored domain of modular exponentiation.

