%% SECTION 3: APPROACH
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Training Approach}

\subsection{Sample generation}\label{sec:samplegen}
Following \citet{charton2024learning}, we experiment with various methods of sampling integers, in our case for modular exponentiation. Unlike GCD, where two integers $a,b \in \mathbb Z$ and their respective $\mathrm{gcd}(a,b)$ are sampled, modular exponentiation requires three operands. The below description focuses on sampling strategies for integers $a,b,c \in \mathbb Z$ and their outcome $d \in \mathbb Z$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dmod}
    a ^ b \equiv d \mod c
\end{equation}
We try to sample $a,b,c$, and sometimes $d$, such that the tuples $(a,b,c,d)$ follow a certain underlying distribution, with the maximum integer to sample set to $M = 10^6$. Similarly to \citet{charton2024learning}, we find that the different distributions lead to varying learning dynamics and absolute accuracies. We observe a clear correlation for the distribution of samples and the accuracy achieved on these numbers, with greater imbalance leading to lower overall accuracy. We detail the sampling generation strategies below.

\paragraph{Uniform operands} The simplest strategy involves uniformly at random sampling $a,b,c$ and computing $d$. This initial approach samples $c \in [1,100]$ and $a,b \in [0, M]$ independently and uniformly at random. $d$ is then computed according to \ref{eq:dmod}. This results in $d \in [0, 99]$, which is heavily skewed towards small values, as can be seen in the histogram of the generated samples \autoref{fig:histogramuniform}.
The decoder might never learn to predict larger outcomes due to the large class imbalance, which we verify in our experiments.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{histogram_uniform.png}
    \caption{Histogram showing the individual parameters of the generated samples in the uniform operands (left) and computed outcome setting (right). Both plots have evenly spaced bins. Note that most $d$ values lie in $[0, 40)$.}
    \label{fig:histogramuniform}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Uniform outcomes} To mitigate this high probability mass for small outcomes, we experiment with sampling the outcome $d$ uniformly at random. For a given $d$, we proceed to rejection sample $(a,b,c)$ independently and uniformly at random. This yields a uniform distribution on all possible triples $(a,b,c)$ such that \ref{eq:dmod} holds. Note that $c > d$, so $c \in (d, 100]$, reducing the search space. The overall distribution obtained is uniform for $d$ and $(a,b,c)$ uniform conditioned on \ref{eq:dmod}.

\paragraph{Reciprocal Operands} As in \citet{charton2024learning}, we experiment with sampling operands according to a reciprocal distribution defined on $[0, M]$. Firstly, we start by sampling $\ln(a), \ln(b)$ according to $\mathcal U(1, ln(M+2))$ and computing $a = e^{\ln(a)}, b = e^{\ln(b)}$, resulting in log-uniform values on $[1, M+2)$. The values are floored to obtain integers in $[1, M+1]$. Note that with this procedure, $a$ and $b$ are enforced to be strictly greater than 0, a constraint that is not enforced with uniform operands.
The reason for this lies in the reciprocal distribution that requires the lower bound to be greater than 0. Since $a,b = 0$ is a well-defined modular exponentiation, these values should be included in our sampling. We address this by shifting the reciprocal distribution by 1. We sample $a',b'$ log-uniformly and set $a = a' - 1, b = b' - 1$. A histogram for reciprocal operands with uniform outcomes is displayed in \autoref{fig:histogramuniformreciprocal}. The resulting individual distributions are uniform $d$, roughly log-uniform $a,b$ and $c$ skewed towards larger values, as expected due to the constraint $c > d$.
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{histogram_uniform_reciprocal.png}
    \caption{Histogram for generated samples in the uniform outcomes, reciprocal operands setting. Note that $a,b$ are given on a log-uniform scale, while the bins for $c,d$ are spaced uniformly.}
    \label{fig:histogramuniformreciprocal}
\end{figure}

The resulting discrete probability distribution $\mathcal P$ that we get for sampling for sampling an integer $n$ is given as
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal P(n, 0, M) = \begin{cases}0 & n < 0 \mathrm{~or~} n \ge + 1\\
    \frac{\ln(n + 2) - \ln(n+1)}{\ln(M+2)} & 0 \le n \le M
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
A detailed derivation is provided in the appendix \ref{append:operanddistribution}. The strategies for uniform outcomes and simply computing the result $d$ are performed both for uniform and reciprocal operands, resulting in four different combinations tested. Note that uniform outcomes can be combined with reciprocal operands by rejection sampling $c$ uniformly and $a,b$ reciprocal.

\subsection{Model training}
For comparability with \citet{charton2024learning}, we train four-layer encoder-decoder transformers with an embedding dimension of 256. Multi-head attention with eight attention heads is used. The batch size is set to 256 and a static learning rate of $10^{-4}$ is given to the Adam optimizer \citep{kingma2014adam}.
Each epoch consists of $300,000$ generated samples, using seed 42. 

\paragraph{Integer representations} Since transformers operate on discrete tokens, and the range of integers up to $M$ is too large to use as a vocabulary for the small transformers trained here, a different representation of integers is needed. Again, this work follows \citet{charton2024learning} in using the following string template, adapted to modular exponentiation
\begin{equation*}
    \mathrm{V3~} + a_1 \ldots a_n + b_1 \ldots b_n + c_1 \ldots c_n + d_1 \ldots d_n
\end{equation*}
where $a_1, a_2, \ldots$ are the individual digits of the number $a$ for a given base $B$. This setting requires $B + 2$ tokens, one for the $+$ sign and an additional one for $\mathrm{V3}$.
For example, the equation $$750178^{996884} \equiv 1 \mod 95$$ is represented in base 1000 as
\begin{equation*}
    \mathrm{V3} +750~178~ +996~884~ +95~ +1.
\end{equation*}
Similarly, for base 1013 the resulting string would be
\begin{equation*}
    \mathrm{V3} +740~558~ +984~92~ +95~ +1.
\end{equation*}
This string is constructed using the samples generated during training.


\paragraph{Sampling}
Subsection \ref{sec:samplegen} proposed four different sample generation methods. All four methods were tested for 2500 epochs with base 1000. Test and validation sets are also generated. Contrary to most works, test accuracy is also measured during training. This is possible as the samples are generated rather than stemming from an existing finite set. Specifically, we generate the two sets
\begin{itemize}
    \item[validation] using uniform operands and computed outcomes.
    \item[test] using uniform operands and uniform outcomes.
\end{itemize}
In this way, we can test the overall model performance for different underlying distributions simultaneously. We report accuracy by comparing the decoder output with the correct value $d$. 

\paragraph{Bases} Since the choice of the base influences the string representation for the transformer, presenting a starting bias, various bases are tested to see whether the choice of the base impacts overall performance. Specifically, four bases are tested on the uniform outcome reciprocal operand setting of Subsection \ref{sec:samplegen}, as the model performance is strongest on this setting. The four bases are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item 1000, as the final result used by \citet{charton2024learning}.
    \item 1013, as a prime number close to 1000, ideally isolating the effect of a prime number without skewing the overall results.
    \item 1279, as the nearest Mersenne prime number.
    \item 999, since the prime factorisation $999 = 3^3 \cdot 37$ has four prime factors as opposed to the 6 by 1000, but both numbers have only 2 distinct prime factors.
\end{itemize}

