\begin{abstract}

% Time series analysis have been used in various real-world applications with deep learning. 
Recently, forecasting future abnormal events has emerged as an important scenario to tackle real-world necessities. 
However, the solution of predicting specific future time points when anomalies will occur, known as Anomaly Prediction (AP), remains under-explored.
% Although AP has gained attention recently, how to solve AP is not studied yet.
Existing methods dealing with time series data fail in AP, focusing only on immediate anomalies or failing to provide precise predictions for future anomalies. 
To address the AP task, we propose a novel framework called Anomaly to Prompt (A2P), comprised of Anomaly-Aware Forecasting (AAF) and Synthetic Anomaly Prompting (SAP). 
To enable the forecasting model to forecast abnormal time points, we adopt a strategy to learn the relationships of anomalies. For the robust detection of anomalies, our proposed SAP introduces a learnable Anomaly Prompt Pool (APP) that simulates diverse anomaly patterns using signal-adaptive prompt. 
% Additionally, we employ a shared backbone architecture to integrate time series representations for both forecasting and anomaly detection. 
Comprehensive experiments on multiple real-world datasets demonstrate the superiority of A2P over state-of-the-art methods, showcasing its ability to predict future anomalies.
Our implementation code is available at \url{https://github.com/KU-VGI/AP}.
\end{abstract}

