\section{Related Works}
\subsection{Jigsaw Puzzles}
The jigsaw puzzle has long been a popular self-supervised task in the computer vision community, aimed at learning visual representations by spatial reasoning and part-whole understanding. \citet{Unsupervised-Learning-of-Visual-Representations-by-Solving-Jigsaw} pioneered this approach by training a CNN to reassemble shuffled image patches, which transfers well to downstream recognition tasks.
Subsequent work extended this idea: JiGen~\citep{Domain-Generalization-by-Solving-Jigsaw-Puzzles} coupled jigsaw puzzles with object classification to boost domain generalization, while Jigsaw Clustering~\citep{Jigsaw-Clustering} blended intra-image permutation recovery with inter-image clustering to match the performance of contrastive learning without the dual-batch overhead.
On the other hand, vision reasoning is still a weak spot for multimodal large language models (MLLMs): the very recent Jigsaw-Puzzles~\citep{Jigsaw-Puzzles} benchmark shows that leading MLLMs score only 30\% on ordering tasks, far below human performance. The contemporary work Jigsaw-R1~\citep{Jigsaw-R1} solves jigsaw puzzles with reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT), showing that RFT can improve MLLMs from near-random to near-perfect performance. 
Collectively, these works mainly treat jigsaw puzzles as a pretext task for representation learning or a test task for probing the vision reasoning capability of MLLMs. However, we employ jigsaw puzzles to investigate how post-training algorithms affect the forgetting behavior of MLLMs.
% Collectively, these studies affirm that jigsaw puzzles provide a powerful yet still under-exploited signal for grounding spatial structure in MLLMs pre-training and fine-tuning.

\subsection{Reinforcement Fine-Tuning in MLLMs}
Inspired by the success of RFT in large language models~\citep{DeepSeek-R1, ouyang2022training},
% , most notably DeepSeek-R1, which demonstrated robust generalization across mathematics, coding, and reasoning tasks, 
recent work has applied RFT to MLLMs. Among them, \citet{MM-Eureka} applies rule-based RFT algorithm to vision–language reasoning tasks, \emph{e.g.}, image-conditioned mathematic and logical problems, and finds better out-of-distribution generalization performance than SFT.
% Meanwhile, several works have observed that for perception-centric tasks—such as image classification, visual grounding, and spatial reasoning—an explicit chain of thought may be superfluous. Instruction-tuned MLLMs often produce answers directly from visual cues, ignoring requests for detailed explanations~\citep{Med-R1, Perception-R1}; enforcing multi-step reasoning in such settings can even degrade performance, as concise answers typically suffice~\citep{MME-CoT, Think-or-Not-Think}. 
Meanwhile, RFT is also employed for perception-centric tasks, such as image classification~\citep{liu2025visual}, visual grounding~\citep{VLM-R1}, and reasoning segmentation~\citep{Seg-Zero}, still conferring notable gains in generalization and robustness when compared with SFT. 
Contemporary studies~\citep{ProRL, VideoChat-R1} show that MLLMs can acquire structured, step-by-step reasoning abilities with carefully designed reward functions. Besides, Jigsaw-R1~\citep{Jigsaw-R1} first applies RFT to the novel task of jigsaw puzzles, but its accuracy remains limited. Our work, as a further step, prolongs the training of RFT to tens of thousands of steps for deeper exploration and achieves significant improvements in final accuracy.
