We also fine-tune Qwen-2.5-VL-3B on the jigsaw puzzle training set using the standard SFT approach. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:main_results_transposed}, due to the property of teacher forcing, the model quickly picks up task-specific patterns under SFT, achieving performance comparable to GRPO after just one epoch.

To assess the impact of SFT and RFT on previously learned knowledge, we further evaluate both models on a set of prior benchmarks in Tab.~\ref{tab:main_results_transposed}. While SFT achieves high accuracy with much less training time, it leads to significantly more catastrophic forgetting than GRPO, which was trained for many more steps. This forgetting is particularly evident on the Grounding task. Moreover, we also evaluate the evolution of perplexity across the post-training stage for jigsaw puzzles and Grounding tasks. As shown in Fig.~\ref{combined_ppl_final}, the perplexity of Grounding samples continually increases during the SFT-GT, while remaining constant during GRPO.

Furthermore, we examined the outputs of \textbf{SFT-GT-1epoch} on the Grounding task manually. After training on the jigsaw puzzles, we observed that the model often switched its output format on Grounding images, \emph{i.e.}, from the expected JSON format containing bbox to a list of numbers as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:rec_change}. This is likely because both the jigsaw and Grounding tasks require outputting numbers in square brackets, which may cause competition in the output space and lead to forgetting. However, why RFT is able to largely avoid this issue is the key question we aim to explore next.

