\subsection{Why does RFT avoid catastrophic forgetting?}\label{sec:rollout_sft}
We start by analyzing the loss function of RFT and SFT. By carefully comparing the gradients of the RFT and SFT losses (Eq.~\ref{gradient_grpo} and Eq.~\ref{gradient_sft}, derivation can be found in Appendix~\ref{sec:connection}), we find that both losses optimize the model's likelihood. However, the difference lies in that RFT optimizes on the dataset sampled by the model and uses adaptive weights for the likelihood objective, while SFT uniformly improves the model's likelihood on a pre-constructed dataset.

Therefore, we investigate whether the corpus sampled from the model itself enables the base model to learn jigsaw puzzles while retaining its performance on previous tasks with SFT. Specifically, we employed the GRPO-trained model to generate responses on the training split of jigsaw puzzles, and filter the responses based on the correctness of the answer, remaining about 60\% of training images. We then use this filtered corpus to fine-tune the base model under the SFT paradigm. To rule out confounding factors, we adopt exactly the same hyper-parameters as in previous SFT experiments.

As shown in Tab.~\ref{tab:main_results_transposed}, fine-tuned on model-generated correct answers, \textbf{SFT-GRPO-Rollout-6epochs} forgets much less than \textbf{SFT-GT-1epoch}, especially on the Grounding task. Interestingly, we also find that training on model-generated correct answers learns much more slowly than on GT data, necessitating 6 epochs to achieve a comparable performance on jigsaw puzzles. This may be because the long reasoning paths dilute the per-token learning signal. In addition, the perplexity of Grounding samples during SFT-GRPO-Rollout also remains small as shown in Fig.~\ref{combined_ppl_final}, sharing a similar tendency as the one in GRPO. Overall, we find that the training data is the key reason why RFT does not suffer from catastrophic forgetting.
