\subsection{What makes the model-generated Data different?}\label{sec:data}
Next, we investigate why the model-generated GRPO corpus and the manually constructed SFT corpus result in such different forgetting behaviors. To do this, we use the \emph{perplexity} of the base model as a measure to compare how well each type of data aligns with the model’s distribution. In general, lower perplexity means the base model is more likely to generate that response.

Specifically, we randomly sample 200 questions and generate 16 rollouts for each using both the base model and the \textbf{GRPO-8epochs} model with a GRPO-style reasoning prompt. For comparison, we also generate 16 rollouts using the base model with a standard SFT prompt. An example is shown in the left and middle panels of Fig.~\ref{fig:ppl_gap_combined}. We observe that the rollouts from \textbf{GRPO-8epochs} tend to align with the lower-perplexity region of the base model’s output distribution, whereas the manually written ground-truth answer typically lies around the median. This suggests that GRPO-generated data is more compatible with the base model’s prior compared to the SFT data. Additional examples are provided in Fig.~\ref{fig:more_gt_dist} and Fig.~\ref{fig:more_grpo_dist} of Appendix \ref{appendix:qualitative}.

To quantify this further, we compute the difference between the median perplexity of the GRPO rollouts and the lower quartile perplexity of the base model for each question. We use these 200 values to estimate the distribution of perplexity gaps. For the SFT data, we compute the perplexity difference in the same way using the ground-truth answers. As shown in the right panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:ppl_gap_combined}, the GRPO distribution is clearly shifted to the left, indicating that GRPO data is overall better aligned with the base model than SFT data. In Fig.~\ref{fig:grpo6_dist} of Appendix~\ref{appendix:qualitative}, we further sample responses from the \textbf{GRPO-6epochs} model and plot their distribution, and the same leftward shift is observed.

