\subsection{Learning Dynamics-based analysis of Pre-training and Post-training} 
Motivated by the observation that the distinct data distributions in the post-training phase lead to different forgetting behaviors, we take a learning dynamics perspective to investigate and explain this phenomenon. Let's first consider the training loss used during the pre-training of MLLMs:
\begin{equation}\label{loss:pretraining}
    \min \mathcal{L}(\theta) = - \mathbf{E}_{q, t\sim\text{Dataset}}\log \pi_\theta (o_t|q, o_{<t}),
\end{equation}
which shares exactly the same formula as the one used in SFT stage. Within Eq.~\ref{loss:pretraining}, $o_t$ is the ground-truth next token, conditioned on the prompt $q$ (including the input images and question) and previous completion $o_{<t}$.

Following \citet{ren2024learning}, we employ learning dynamics to describe ``how the change in parameter $\theta$ induced by a step of gradient descent on single training example $\textcolor{red}{x_u} \triangleq \{q^u, o_{<t}^u, o_t^u\}$ impacts the probability of another example $\textcolor{blue}{x_v} \triangleq \{q^v, o_{<t}^v, o_t^v\}$''. Here, we treat $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$ as a sample from the pre-training data, and $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ as a target example of interest—such as a sample simulated by GRPO or a man-made SFT sample from jigsaw puzzles. Specifically, we have
\begin{align}
    &\Delta\theta (\textcolor{red}{x_u})\triangleq\theta' - \theta = \eta \cdot \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta (\textcolor{red}{x_u})= \eta \cdot \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta (o^u_t|q^u, o^u_{<t})\label{eq:gd};
     \\&\Delta \log\pi(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})|_{\textcolor{red}{x_u}}\triangleq \log\pi_{\theta'}(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})-\log\pi_{\theta}(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})=\log\pi_{\theta'}(o_t^v|q^v, o_{<t}^v)-\log\pi_{\theta}(o_t^v|q^v, o_{<t}^v)\label{eq:def_learning_dynamics}.
\end{align}
And we want to specify the relationship between $\Delta\theta (\textcolor{red}{x_u})$ and $\Delta \log\pi(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})|_{\textcolor{red}{x_u}}$. 

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{Decomposition}\label{theo:decomp} Let $\pi_\theta(x)=\Softmax(\vz(x))[o_t]\in[0,1]$, where $\vz(x)=h_\theta(q, o_{<t})\in \mathbb{R}^{V}$, $V$ is the number of tokens within vocabulary. The one-step learning dynamics decompose has the following format:
    \begin{equation}
        \underbrace{
            \Delta \log\pi(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})|_{\textcolor{red}{x_u}}
        }_{1\times 1} 
        = \eta
        \underbrace{
            \mathcal{A}(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})
        }_{1\times V}
        \underbrace{
            \mathcal{K}(\textcolor{blue}{x_v},\textcolor{red}{x_u})
        }_{V\times V}
        \underbrace{
            \mathcal{G}(\textcolor{red}{x_u})
        }_{V\times 1}
            + \bigO(\eta^2),
    \end{equation}
    where $\mathcal{A}(\textcolor{blue}{x_v}) =\nabla_{\vz}\log\pi_{\theta}(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})$, $\mathcal{K}(\textcolor{blue}{x_v},\textcolor{red}{x_u}) = (\nabla_{\theta}\vz(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})|_{\theta})(\nabla_{\theta}\vz(\textcolor{red}{x_u})|_{\theta})^\top$ is the empirical neural tangent kernel (eNTK) of the logit network $\vz$,
    and $\mathcal{G}(\textcolor{red}{x_u})=\nabla_{\vz}\log\pi_{\theta}(\textcolor{red}{x_u})$.
\end{restatable}

Proof of the theorem and more discussion about learning dynamics can be found in Appendix~\ref{sec:learning_dynamics}.

Our analysis in this section also depends on the assumption of ``the relative influence of learning $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$ on other inputs $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$, \emph{i.e.}, the eNTK matrix $\mathcal{K}$, remains roughly stable over training'', which is well-validated in \citet{ren2024learning}. As we showed in Sec.~\ref{sec:data}, the base model already contains hidden “knowledge” for the novel task—such as GRPO rollouts—that have low perplexity, even though it was only pre-trained on massive amounts of unrelated data $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$. This suggests that learning general knowledge during pre-training can still benefit specific examples $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ that are suitable for novel tasks by accident. But then, during post-training, what happens if we further strengthen this helpful knowledge discovered by RFT for the new task? Will it affect the previously learned knowledge from $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$? Fortunately, we can answer this using the following symmetry property from learning dynamics:

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{SymmetricLearningDynamics}\label{theo:symmetry} The one-step learning dynamics has the property of symmetry:
    \begin{equation}
        \Delta\log\pi(\textcolor{blue}{x_v})|_{\textcolor{red}{x_u}} = \Delta\log\pi(\textcolor{red}{x_u})|_{\textcolor{blue}{x_v}}+ \bigO(\eta^2).
    \end{equation}
\end{restatable}

% Equipped with theorems of learning dynamics, let's start the analysis of the forgetting behavior of RFT and SFT. We can treat $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$ as the data used during the pretraining of MLLM, and $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ as the correct responses for a novel task we collect during the RFT process that should be learned during post-training. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:ppl_gap_combined}~(a), after pretraining on massive $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$, $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ already has relatively high likelihood (\emph{i.e.}, low perplexity) when conditioned on the corresponding prompts. This suggests that learning $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$ during pretraining positively influences the likelihood of $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$.

According to Theorem~\ref{theo:symmetry} (Proof in Appendix~\ref{sec:learning_dynamics}), we find that the influence of learning $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$ on $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ is nearly the same as the influence of learning $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ on $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$. This means that during post-training, repeatedly learning and reinforcing certain low-perplexity examples—such as those identified by the RFT algorithm—can actually help preserve or even enhance the pre-trained knowledge $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$. This also explains the observation in Sec.~\ref{sec:rollout_sft}: the perplexity of Grounding-related knowledge does not degrade during GRPO or SFT GRPO-Rollout training.

% Moreover, since the influence of learning $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$ on other responses $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ remains relatively stable during training~\citep{arora2019exact,jacot2018neural,ren2024learning}, and based on the symmetry of influence established in Theorem~\ref{theo:symmetry}, we find that increasing the likelihood of high-probability samples like $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ in the post-training stage can also positively affect the associated pre-trained knowledge $\textcolor{red}{x_u}$. This explains our observation in Fig.~\ref{combined_ppl_final}: the perplexity of Grounding-related tokens continues to decrease during GRPO or SFT on GRPO rollouts. Therefore, learning this hidden ' knowledge' does not cause severe catastrophic forgetting.

However, because the total output probability must sum to one, aggressively enhancing new knowledge $\textcolor{blue}{x_v}$ can still degrade the perplexity of other useful knowledge. This aligns with the slight performance drop on some tasks, \emph{e.g.}, \textbf{Document \& OCR}, as shown in Tab.~\ref{tab:main_results_transposed}.

