\section{Related Works}

% Calibration methods can be broadly categorized into three main approaches: non-parametric, parametric, and ensemble-based techniques. Non-parametric methods offer flexibility by making minimal assumptions about underlying distributions, though often requiring more validation data. Parametric methods employ predefined functional forms with trainable parameters, providing computational efficiency and mathematical interpretability. Ensemble-based approaches leverage multiple models or outputs to approximate Bayesian inference for improved uncertainty estimation. Each approach presents distinct advantages and limitations for different calibration scenarios.


\paragraph{Non-parametric and Parametric Calibration}
Calibration methods can be broadly categorized into two main approaches: non-parametric and parametric techniques. Non-parametric methods offer flexibility without assuming specific functional forms, including Histogram Binning (HB) \citep{zadrozny2001obtaining}, which partitions confidence scores into bins, and its Bayesian extension BBQ \citep{naeini2015obtaining}. Spline calibration \citep{gupta2021calibration} fits piecewise cubic functions for smooth mappings between uncalibrated and calibrated probabilities, handling sparse data regions better than isotonic regression. However, these methods often require more validation data and may alter prediction rankings. Parametric methods, in contrast, adjust model outputs through predefined functional forms. TS \citep{guo2017calibration} applies a single scalar to logits, while enhanced variants include PTS \citep{tomani2022parameterized}, which uses neural networks for sample-specific temperatures, and CTS \citep{frenkel2021network}, which implements class-wise adjustments. Group Calibration \citep{yang2024beyond} and ProCal \citep{xiong2023proximityinformed} target multi-calibration \citep{hebert2018multicalibration} by grouping similar samples. Train-time approaches, including Brier Loss \citep{brier1950verification}, Dirichlet Scaling \citep{kull2019beyond}, MMCE \citep{kumar2018trainable}, Label Smoothing \citep{szegedy2016rethinking}, and Focal Loss variants \citep{mukhoti2020calibrating, tao2023dual}, typically incur higher computational costs.

\paragraph{Ensemble-Based Calibration}
Ensemble-based calibration methods combine multiple models or outputs to approximate Bayesian inference for improved uncertainty estimation. Deep ensembles \citep{lakshminarayanan2017simple} provide a scalable alternative to Bayesian Neural Networks, while dropout-based approaches \citep{gal2016dropout} leverage stochasticity as approximate Bayesian inference. Data-augmentation ensembles \citep{conde2023approaching} improve calibration through test-time transforms, and Ensemble-based Temperature Scaling (ETS) \citep{zhang2020mix} applies Bayesian principles to adjust temperatures. Though Effective, these methods typically demand significant computational resources for training multiple models or performing repeated inferences. Conversely, our approach achieves superior calibration through more efficient means.




\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

In this section, we first summarize preliminaries in Sec. \ref{subsec:preliminaries}. Next, we introduce a scaling adapter in Sec. \ref{subsec:our_scaling} that learns to reduce calibration error using a logit gap indicator. We then analyze sample-wise objectives in Sec. \ref{subsec:softbin_ece} and introduce a relaxed accuracy estimation for stable temperature learning. Finally, we provide theoretical justification in Sec. \ref{subsec:theoretical_evidence}.


\subsection{Preliminaries}
\label{subsec:preliminaries}
A classification model is considered \textit{calibrated} if its predictive confidence matches its actual accuracy. Formally, for a classifier $f_{\theta}$ and input $\mathbf{x}$ with true label $y$, perfect calibration implies $\mathbb{P}(y=\hat{y} \mid p_{\theta}(\hat{y} \mid \mathbf{x})=p) = p$ for all confidence values $p \in [0,1]$, where $\hat{y}$ is the predicted class.

\textbf{Expected Calibration Error (ECE)}\quad
Consider a classification model $f_{\theta}$ producing logits $\mathbf{z}_i \in \mathbb{R}^K$ for a given input $\mathbf{x}_i$, where $K$ is the number of classes. After applying a softmax function, we obtain the predictive probability for class $k$ as $p_{\theta}(y_i = k \mid \mathbf{x}_i)= \frac{\exp(z_{i,k})}{\sum_{j=1}^K \exp(z_{i,j})}$,
% \begin{equation}
% p_{\theta}(y_i = k \mid \mathbf{x}_i)
% = \frac{\exp(z_{i,k})}{\sum_{j=1}^K \exp(z_{i,j})},
% \label{eq:softmax}
% \end{equation}
where $z_{i,k}$ is the $k$-th component of $\mathbf{z}_i$.
To quantify calibration error, we use the \emph{Expected Calibration Error} (ECE). In a standard binning scheme, we partition samples into $B$ bins based on their predicted confidence, compute the average accuracy and confidence within each bin, and measure the difference. Formally, let $\hat{p}_b$ be the average confidence and $\hat{a}_b$ the empirical accuracy in bin $b$. The ECE is:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{ECE} = \sum_{b=1}^{B} \frac{|I_b|}{N} \bigl| \hat{p}_b - \hat{a}_b \bigr|,
\end{equation}
where $I_b$ is the set of indices in bin $b$ and $N$ is the total number of samples. 
%While hard binning is frequently employed, it induces undesirable discontinuities and lacks compatibility with gradient-based optimization.

\textbf{Standard Temperature Scaling}\quad Temperature scaling introduces a scalar $T > 0$ to adjust the logit distribution before the softmax:
\begin{equation}
p_{\theta,T}(y_i = k \mid \mathbf{x}_i)
= \frac{\exp\bigl(z_{i,k} / T\bigr)}{\sum_{j=1}^K \exp\bigl(z_{i,j} / T\bigr)}.
\label{eq:temp_scaling}
\end{equation}
A smaller temperature $T < 1$ sharpens the distribution, while a larger temperature $T > 1$ flattens it.
Vanilla temperature scaling searches for a global $\hat{T}$ using a held-out validation set $\mathcal{D}_{val}$:
\begin{equation}
\hat{T} = \arg\min_{T>0} \ \mathcal{L}_{\text{calib}}(\mathcal{D}_{val}, f_{\theta}, T),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_{\text{calib}}$ is a chosen calibration metric such as negative log-likelihood or ECE.
While effective at shifting the overall confidence distribution, a single global temperature fails to capture the \textit{varying degrees} of miscalibration across different samples. This creates a substantial gap in achieving fine-grained calibration, as demonstrated by recent works \cite{zhang2020mix, mukhoti2020calibrating, kull2019beyond}. This limitation has motivated various extensions, including class-wise temperature scaling \cite{kull2019beyond} and parametric approaches \cite{zhang2020mix}, but they still do not fully leverage sample-specific calibration signals.

\subsection{Scaling with Logit Gaps}
\label{subsec:our_scaling}

Current post-hoc calibration methods face several limitations. HB and its variants often do not preserve prediction results and lack interpretability. TS applies a uniform adjustment to all samples, failing to accurately calibrate confidence at a fine-grained level. While class-wise TS and matrix scaling add flexibility, they risk altering the original model's predictions.
Recent advanced methods have explored sample-wise temperature scaling by leveraging information in embedded features or model predictions \cite{zhang2020mix, rahimi2020intra}. However, these features were not designed specifically to provide uncertainty information but rather to assist in classification. Furthermore, for large-scale datasets with numerous classes, the increased dimensionality introduces substantial noise for precise temperature parameterization, requiring larger calibration sample sets.
The crucial problem is:
how can we precisely scale the output logits for calibration, while maintaining the invariance in model prediction?

Aiming at this goal, we propose to leverage a \textit{single scalar} as a denoised signal from the logit space of possibly thousands of classes. Specifically, let
\begin{align}
z_{i,\text{max}} = \max_{k \in [K]} z_{i,k}, \quad
z_{i,\text{2nd}} = \max_{k \in [K] \setminus \{\arg\max_k z_{i,k}\}} z_{i,k}
\end{align}
Then we define the \textbf{logit gap} for sample $i$ as
\begin{equation}
g_i = z_{i,\text{max}} - z_{i,\text{2nd}}.
\label{eq:logit_gap}
\end{equation}
% Logit gap indicates confidence.
Intuitively, a model generally exhibits higher confidence in a specific sample when there exists a substantial gap between the largest and the second-largest logits. Conversely, smaller logit gaps indicate greater uncertainty for harder samples that possibly require different confidence adjustments. 

Unlike high-dimensional embeddings or complex uncertainty proxies, this simple measure directly arises from the classifier's decision boundary and naturally reflects prediction stability. %prediction confidence.
It is worth noting that the predicted confidence or the maximum logit themselves cannot indicate such knowledge, as they only carry intra-class signals.


\paragraph{Light-weight Temperature Regression}
While current approaches emphasize on network expressiveness \cite{tomani2022parameterized} to capture complex patterns in mapping the full logit distributions to temperature shifts, they become over-parameterized for processing the proposed logit gaps, as the learned scalar-to-scalar relationship is simple and direct. Aiming at devising a minimal but precise parameterization design, we model temperature regression $h_\phi(\cdot): \mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ as a single hidden layer MLP, \textit{i.e.},
\begin{equation}
\hat{T}=h_\phi(g_i) = \operatorname{softplus}(\mathbf{W}_2 \cdot \operatorname{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_1 \cdot g_i + \mathbf{b}_1) + b_2) + \epsilon,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{W}_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{d\times 1}$, $\mathbf{W}_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{1\times d}$, $\mathbf{b}_1\in\mathbb R^d$, and $b_2\in\mathbb R$ are learnable parameters and the hidden dimension $d$ is set to $16$ in our implementation. The softplus activation and $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ ensure positive temperatures with numerical stability. Though simple, such a restrained design is built on the fact that a desired temperature value $T_i$ is tightly bounded by $g_i$ as analyzed in Sec.~\ref{subsec:theoretical_evidence} theoretically.
Our parameterization enables effective temperature adjustment with substantially fewer parameters of a fixed $3d+1=49$ values, while the parameter size of PTS \cite{tomani2022parameterized} (two hidden layers of size 5 on the top-10 logits) is a fixed $91$ values \cite{tomani2022parameterized}, CTS \cite{guo2017calibration} learns $C$ temperatures, vector scaling \cite{kull2019beyond} uses $2C$ parameters, matrix scaling or Dirichlet calibration \cite{kull2019beyond} incur $C^2 + C$ parameters, and Spline \citep{gupta2021calibration} results in $13C$ parameters. For ImageNet ($C=1,000$), these latter approaches become ill-shaped and inefficient to learn precise confidence adjustments.




\subsection{Precise Temperature Mapping with Soft-Bin ECE}
\label{subsec:softbin_ece}

Current post-hoc calibration methods face a fundamental bias-variance tradeoff: they either use global objectives with minimal parameterization (high bias) or point-wise objectives that introduce substantial variance through binary-valued dependent variables. Following \cite{liu2024optimizing}, we reformulate the problem as regression of model accuracy given confidence distribution:

\begin{align}
\min_{\phi} \int \|\hat{p}-\mathbb{E}^{\text{acc}}_{\hat{p}}\|dp(\hat{p})
=\int \Big\|\hat{p}-\int \mathbb{I}\{y_\mathbf{x}=\hat{y}_\mathbf{x}\}dp(\mathbf{x}|\hat{p})\Big\|dp(\hat{p}),
\label{eq:general_problem}
\end{align}

Due to intractable distributions $p(\mathbf{x}|\hat{p})$, existing methods rely on approximations. Global methods use overall accuracy $\mathbb{E}^{\text{global}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}\{y_i=\hat{y}_i\}$, introducing substantial bias while permitting only minimal parameterization. In contrast, sample-wise methods \cite{tomani2022parameterized, liu2024optimizing} use point-level approximation $\mathbb{E}^{\text{point}}_{\hat{x_i}}=\mathbb{I}\{y_i=\hat{y}_i\}$, which introduces high variance from binary-valued dependent variables, necessitating heavier parameterization.

To balance bias and variance, we divide the confidence space using range-wise discrete bins with a membership function:

\begin{equation}
P(b|\hat{p}_i) = w_{i,b} = \frac{\exp\Bigl(-\alpha \bigl(\hat{p}_i - c_b\bigr)^2\Bigr)}{\sum_{b'=1}^B \exp\Bigl(-\alpha \bigl(\hat{p}_i - c_{b'}\bigr)^2\Bigr)},
\end{equation}

where $c_b$ is the bin center and $\alpha > 0$ controls weighting smoothness. The estimated model accuracy per bin becomes:

\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}^{\text{soft}}_{b}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{i,b} \,\mathbb{I} \{y_i = \hat{y}_i\} \Big/ \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{i,b}.
\end{equation}

Notably, as $\alpha \rightarrow 0^+$, $\mathbb{E}^{\text{soft}}_{b}$ degenerates to $\mathbb{E}^{\text{global}}$, while setting $b \rightarrow 0$ produces $\mathbb{E}^{\text{point}}_{\hat{x_i}}$. Thus, $\alpha$ and $b$ serve as natural bias-variance knobs, enabling both parameter efficiency and precise confidence estimation. This approach distributes each prediction's influence across neighboring bins, creating smooth gradients throughout the confidence spectrum and yielding the soft-binned ECE \cite{karandikar2021soft} objective:

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SoftECE}(\mathcal{D}_{val}, \theta) = \Bigg(\sum_{b=1}^{B}
\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{i,b}}{N}
\left|\mathbb{E}^{\text{soft}}_{\hat{p}}
-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{i,b} \,\hat{p}_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{i,b}}\right|^q\Bigg)^{1/q},
\label{eq:softbin_ece}
\end{equation}

Figure~\ref{fig:intro}(a, c) demonstrates SoftECE's superior performance across varying parameter counts and validation set sizes, maintaining its advantage even with just 0.1\% of the dataset for validation. As shown in Section \ref{Ablation Studies}, this balanced approach delivers consistent performance across diverse architectures and datasets. Algorithm in Appendix~\ref{Appendix:SMART_framework} details our implementation. % using SoftECE as the optimization objective.




\subsection{Theoretical Evidence for Logit Gaps}
\label{subsec:theoretical_evidence}
We theoretically explain the choice of logit gap $g_i$ as the prediction output's scalar proxy. Despite the direct scaling effect caused by temperature adjustments, the essence of post-hoc calibration actually lies in precisely controlling the confidence values. Given a specific target confidence, we argue that the maximum logit, despite indicating the confidence before scaling, can hardly bound the best temperature. As a contrast, the devised indicator of logit gap can effectively make a tight bound of such temperature values.
Specifically, given a target confidence value $\hat{p}$, the parameterization model and the soft-ECE objective aims to search a temperature value $T$ subject to
\begin{equation}
\frac{\exp(z_{\text{max}}/T)}{\sum_{j=1}^{K} exp(z_j/T)}=\hat{p},
\label{eq:temp_conf_relations}
\end{equation}
where we assume $\hat{p} \in (0, 1)$ is a fixed value (\textit{e.g.}, $0.82$) determined by minimizing our Soft-ECE objective for each sample $\mathbf{z}$. The sample index subscript is omitted here and subsequently for brevity. The logit gap's theoretical superiority in confidence adjustment is demonstrated by two propositions:

\begin{proposition}[Unbounded Temperature]
\label{prop:unbound}
The implicit function $T=\beta(\mathbf{z})$ defined by Eq. \eqref{eq:temp_conf_relations} is unbounded when fixing $z_{max}$, with $T$ taking values in $(0, +\infty)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[$g$-Boundedness]
\label{prop:g-bound}
$T$ is bounded by $-g/\log(\frac{1-\hat{p}}{\hat{p}})<T\le -g/\log(\frac{1-\hat{p}}{\hat{p}(n-1)})$.
\end{proposition}

Therefore, when $\hat{p}>0.5$, which takes up most cases, our proposed logit gap indicator can effectively determine the temperature when receiving gradients for altering the confidence. A numerical verification is illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:numerical_study}, where logit gaps outperform other scalar indicators (a-c) and facilitate both convergence and performance compared to full logit values (d), statistically validating the superiority of logit gaps in indicating the temperature adjustment. Full proofs are in Appendix~\ref{Appendix:Theoretical_Proofs}.


